            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-66564",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "github.com/sigstore/timestamp-authority: Sigstore Timestamp Authority: Denial of Service via excessive OID or Content-Type header parsing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Sigstore Timestamp Authority is a service for issuing RFC 3161 timestamps. Prior to 2.0.3, Function api.ParseJSONRequest currently splits (via a call to strings.Split) an optionally-provided OID (which is untrusted data) on periods. Similarly, function api.getContentType splits the Content-Type header (which is also untrusted data) on an application string. As a result, in the face of a malicious request with either an excessively long OID in the payload containing many period characters or a malformed Content-Type header, a call to api.ParseJSONRequest or api.getContentType incurs allocations of O(n) bytes (where n stands for the length of the function's argument). This vulnerability is fixed in 2.0.3."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-66564",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-66564\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: github.com/sigstore/timestamp-authority\nFixed Version: 2.0.3\nLink: [CVE-2025-66564](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-66564)\nSigstore Timestamp Authority is a service for issuing RFC 3161 timestamps. Prior to 2.0.3, Function api.ParseJSONRequest currently splits (via a call to strings.Split) an optionally-provided OID (which is untrusted data) on periods. Similarly, function api.getContentType splits the Content-Type header (which is also untrusted data) on an application string. As a result, in the face of a malicious request with either an excessively long OID in the payload containing many period characters or a malformed Content-Type header, a call to api.ParseJSONRequest or api.getContentType incurs allocations of O(n) bytes (where n stands for the length of the function's argument). This vulnerability is fixed in 2.0.3.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-66564**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|github.com/sigstore/timestamp-authority|2.0.3|[CVE-2025-66564](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-66564)|\n\nSigstore Timestamp Authority is a service for issuing RFC 3161 timestamps. Prior to 2.0.3, Function api.ParseJSONRequest currently splits (via a call to strings.Split) an optionally-provided OID (which is untrusted data) on periods. Similarly, function api.getContentType splits the Content-Type header (which is also untrusted data) on an application string. As a result, in the face of a malicious request with either an excessively long OID in the payload containing many period characters or a malformed Content-Type header, a call to api.ParseJSONRequest or api.getContentType incurs allocations of O(n) bytes (where n stands for the length of the function's argument). This vulnerability is fixed in 2.0.3."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 7.5,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
